            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Let \\\\( x \\\\) and \\\\( y \\\\) be positive integers. If for each positive integer \\\\( n \\\\) we have that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(n y)^{2}+1 \\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\n\\\\]\\n\\nprove that \\\\( x=1 \\\\).\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"bmo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Assume for contradiction that \\\\(x>1\\\\). Taking \\\\(n=2\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n4y^2+1\\\\mid x-1.\\n\\\\]\\nChoose a prime \\\\(p\\\\mid 4y^2+1\\\\). This number is odd, and \\\\(3\\\\nmid 4y^2+1\\\\), since \\\\(4y^2+1\\\\equiv y^2+1\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod3\\\\). Hence \\\\(p\\\\ge5\\\\). Set\\n\\\\[\\na=v_p(x-1)\\\\ge1\\n\\\\]\\nand choose an integer \\\\(k>a\\\\).\\n\\nLet \\\\(f(T)=y^2T^2+1\\\\). We have \\\\(f(2)\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod p\\\\), while\\n\\\\[\\nf'(2)=4y^2\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nThus, by the elementary Hensel lifting argument, there exists \\\\(r\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\nr\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod p,\\\\qquad f(r)\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod{p^k}.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently \\\\(s=-r\\\\) also satisfies \\\\(f(s)\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod{p^k}\\\\), and \\\\(s\\\\equiv-2\\\\equiv p-2\\\\pmod p\\\\).\\n\\nPut \\\\(b=(p-1)^2\\\\). Since\\n\\\\[\\nb-1=p(p-2),\\n\\\\]\\nwe have \\\\(v_p(b-1)=1\\\\). By LTE,\\n\\\\[\\nv_p(b^m-1)=1+v_p(m)\\n\\\\]\\nfor every positive integer \\\\(m\\\\). Hence \\\\(b\\\\) has order \\\\(p^{k-1}\\\\) modulo \\\\(p^k\\\\), so it generates the subgroup of units congruent to \\\\(1\\\\pmod p\\\\).\\n\\nNow \\\\(s(p-2)^{-1}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Therefore, for some \\\\(t\\\\ge0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nb^t\\\\equiv s(p-2)^{-1}\\\\pmod{p^k}.\\n\\\\]\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nn=(p-2)b^t=(p-2)(p-1)^{2t}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen \\\\(n\\\\equiv s\\\\pmod{p^k}\\\\), and hence\\n\\\\[\\np^k\\text{ divides }n^2y^2+1.\\n\\\\]\\nEvery prime divisor of \\\\(n\\\\) divides either \\\\(p-2\\\\) or \\\\(p-1\\\\), so it is strictly smaller than \\\\(p\\\\). From the prime-factor formula for Euler’s totient, it follows that\\n\\\\[\\np\\\\nmid\\\\varphi(n).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBy the hypothesis,\\n\\\\[\\nn^2y^2+1\\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(p^k\\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\\\). On the other hand, since \\\\(p\\\\) is odd and \\\\(p\\\\mid x-1\\\\), LTE gives\\n\\\\[\\nv_p\\\\bigl(x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\\\bigr)\\n=v_p(x-1)+v_p(\\\\varphi(n))=a<k,\\n\\\\]\\na contradiction. Therefore \\\\(x>1\\\\) is impossible. Since \\\\(x\\\\) is a positive integer, \\\\(x=1\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
